// The fortress towns' writing (GDD §3.1; App. F.1 T133 and the director's notes of 7 Oct 2026; ARCHITECTURE §8 note 281).
// The numbers (how many people, houses, reach) are in tuning/towns.json; this file is only words, read by
// Sim/Towns/TownGenerator.
//
// The director, 7 Oct: "these are maritime Canada towns ... lore is through NPC dialogue. They should say creepy things,
// sometimes hints of how to deal with monsters they've heard about but never the full rule. Cultures should all be
// traumatized somewhat by the events of the world. Towns should land somewhere between 20 to 350 people."
//
// How a town is written:
// - It's a Maritime railway town (Nova Scotia, New Brunswick, the Island): clapboard and shingle, a white church, the
//   wharf gone quiet, Gaelic and Acadian and Lunenburg names. The voice is plain, dry, a little Maritime ("b'y", "some
//   cold", "the mainland", "from away"), never put on.
// - GDD §2: "the Corruption exaggerates whatever allows something to survive". A town did the same. Every town lost
//   people to one creature, kept the thing that saved the rest, and does it harder every year: its custom. The custom
//   is grief with a rule in it, and the town is not well.
// - People hint at a creature's rule, never say it whole: what happened, who lived and why, what they do now. The crew
//   put it together. They name the creatures in their own words ("the little lights", "the thin one"), never the game's.
// - Mystery is seeded, not explained (App. F.1).
//
// Each culture's creature is by enemies.json "costs" names ("choir" is always about); an edition only gets the customs
// of what it fields. Text is written in sentence case and drawn in the HUD's capitals. Keep a person's line under ~150
// characters and a paper under ~420 (TownTests holds both). Placeholders: {town} this town, {other} another town (from
// "places"), {hall} the custom's building, {law} the town's law, {name}/{name2} somebody else in this town, {family} the
// household's surname, {absent} the one the household lost, {n} a small number, {population} and {former} the town's
// people now and before.
{
  // Given names, the old-fashioned way. Surnames by the province's people: Highland Scots, Acadian, Irish, Lunenburg
  // German, Loyalist English. A household shares one.
  "firstNames": ["Agnes", "Angus", "Annie", "Archie", "Aurèle", "Bernadette", "Bridget", "Catherine", "Christie", "Clarence",
    "Donald", "Dougal", "Edna", "Effie", "Elmer", "Ephraim", "Evangeline", "Flora", "Florence", "Gus", "Hector", "Ida", "Isaac",
    "Jean-Guy", "Johnena", "Josephine", "Lauchie", "Léonie", "Lottie", "Margaret", "Marie-Rose", "Mary-Catherine", "Murdoch",
    "Myrtle", "Nellie", "Obadiah", "Ronald", "Rémi", "Seamus", "Theresa", "Thelma", "Wilfred", "Winnie", "Zita"],
  "surnames": ["MacDonald", "MacNeil", "MacLean", "MacIsaac", "MacKinnon", "Gillis", "Beaton", "Chisholm", "Rankin", "Fraser",
    "Campbell", "Cameron", "Boudreau", "LeBlanc", "Cormier", "Gallant", "Arsenault", "Doucet", "Comeau", "Thibodeau", "Robichaud",
    "Landry", "Murphy", "Power", "Walsh", "Kelly", "O'Brien", "Sweeney", "Hiltz", "Zwicker", "Mosher", "Corkum", "Ernst",
    "Himmelman", "Hatfield", "Crowell", "Nickerson", "Saunders", "Matheson", "Ross"],
  // Other towns, for {other}: down the line, up the shore, gone quiet.
  "places": ["Ship Harbour", "Pell's Cove", "Lower Kettering", "Port Gillis", "Saint-Ambroise", "Glen Morrow", "Cape Sorrow",
    "Upper Blackwell", "Harrow Mills", "Grieve's Brook", "Little Narrows", "Pointe-à-Doucet", "Halloway Station", "Sallow Head",
    "Moss Settlement", "Voss Crossing", "Barrow Bay", "New Kettle"],

  // What the plaque at the way in says: the town, when it was walled, and how many live there, the old number crossed
  // out. One a town.
  "founded": ["Walled the winter after the fall.", "Held since the second winter.", "Never taken. Not all the way.",
    "Built round the old goods yard.", "Rebuilt twice. Not a third time.", "Walled by hand, by the ones who lived."],
  "plaque": ["{TOWN}. {founded} Painted under it, POP. {former}, crossed through. {population} chalked beside it, and rubbed at.",
    "{TOWN}. {founded} The population's painted on a board: {former}. The board's been turned over and {population} written on the back."],

  // The gatekeeper's first words: the town's law, the one thing anyone here tells a stranger.
  "welcome": ["Welcome to {town}. {law} That's all we ask.", "{town}. {law} Say it back. ... Good.",
    "{law} That's {town}'s way. The next town'll laugh at you for it. Let them.", "You're in {town} now. {law} Do it while you're here."],

  "cultures": [
    {
      "id": "hush", "creature": "choir",
      "law": "No singing in {town}. Not even at the burying.",
      "hall": "St. Columba's", "hallStyle": "church",
      "centrepiece": { "kind": "bell", "name": "the church bell",
        "text": "The church bell, taken down and set in a frame, its clapper bound in felt and wire. A row of names is scratched round the rim. The names stop halfway round." },
      "lines": [
        "We had a choir once. Thirty voices. You could hear them down at the wharf.",
        "Mrs. Beaton played the organ. She'd stopped to turn the page. That's the only reason she's still with us.",
        "It took the tenors first. I don't know why I remember that. The tenors.",
        "Don't hum. You're humming. Don't.",
        "My girl sang in her sleep for a year after. We'd sit up and hold her mouth shut. Gently.",
        "Some nights you can hear it out past the wall, trying the tune. It's got most of it now.",
        "The louder you are, the sooner. That's all I know about it, and I wish I didn't.",
        "Shut that door, would you. There's a draught. There's always a draught."
      ],
      "notes": [
        { "title": "No singing", "text": "By order of the council: no singing, whistling or humming within the walls of {town}. This includes hymns. This includes lullabies. Especially lullabies." },
        { "title": "Order of service", "text": "A printed order of service, the hymn numbers crossed out. In pencil underneath: they were on the third verse." },
        { "title": "Found in a coat", "text": "It went right past the vestry. The vestry door was shut." }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "pairs", "creature": "tippyToesie",
      "law": "Nobody sleeps alone in {town}. Nobody waits alone.",
      "hall": "the Mission Hall", "hallStyle": "hall",
      "centrepiece": { "kind": "post", "name": "the waiting post",
        "text": "A post hung with iron rings, short cords knotted to each, worn smooth from wrists. There are more rings than there are people left to tie to them." },
      "lines": [
        "Mam stood at the sink every night of her life. One night she stood there too long.",
        "They found him at the window with his hand still on the curtain. He'd been watching for the train.",
        "If you have to wait, keep moving. Little circles. Look behind you on the turn.",
        "Those of us with nobody sleep in rows in the Mission Hall. Holding hands. You get used to the snoring.",
        "It comes up so soft. You'd never hear it. You'd only feel the hand.",
        "{name} won't stand still even asleep. Her feet go all night under the blanket.",
        "Don't stand there like that. Turn round. Go on. Turn round.",
        "Two can pull it off you. One can't. One just goes quiet."
      ],
      "notes": [
        { "title": "Bench rules", "text": "Two to a bench. One on a bench will be moved on by the warden, for their own sake." },
        { "title": "Lost: one half", "text": "Answers to Edna. Last seen at the pump, standing. If you see her standing, don't come at her from behind. Say her name first." }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "count", "creature": "passenger",
      "law": "Everyone answers to their name at supper.",
      "hall": "the Counting House", "hallStyle": "hall",
      "centrepiece": { "kind": "tally", "name": "the tally board",
        "text": "A blackboard of names, tonight's marks chalked against them. There's one more mark than there are names. Someone's rubbed it out. It's back." },
      "lines": [
        "Say your name. Out loud. ... Thank you. We had one once that wouldn't.",
        "The 41 came in with one more crew than it left with. Nobody could say which. They all said it wasn't them.",
        "It sat at the end of our table all through supper. Didn't eat. Didn't say grace. In the morning the Corkums were gone.",
        "It looks like someone you know. That's the worst of it. Someone you'd trust with your life.",
        "Count your crew. Then count again with your eyes shut, by their voices.",
        "My brother came back from the mill and never said another word. We kept him a year before we were sure.",
        "We lay one place more than we need. Just to see who sits.",
        "Keep talking while I count. No, keep talking. I want to hear you."
      ],
      "notes": [
        { "title": "The count", "text": "All persons within the walls will answer by name at the supper bell. Nodding is not answering." },
        { "title": "From the night mail's log", "text": "Left {other} with nine. Counted ten at the water stop. Counted nine at the bridge. Nobody got off." }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "shutters", "creature": "fireFlies",
      "law": "No light in a window. Not one.",
      "hall": "the Lamp Room", "hallStyle": "hall",
      "centrepiece": { "kind": "lamps", "name": "the shuttered lamps",
        "text": "Racks of lanterns, every glass shuttered in tin. A child's lantern hangs at the end, its shutter soldered shut." },
      "lines": [
        "Pretty little lights, they were. Came right up to the glass. The children waved at them.",
        "The whole east end went up. Burned blue. You could read by it from the wharf.",
        "Our children learn the town in the dark. Count the steps to the well. Count them back.",
        "Stood still with a lamp lit, God help you. Moving, maybe not. Maybe.",
        "Your cars are lit like a dance hall. They'll love that.",
        "I sit in the dark most nights. You get so you can hear the lamps thinking about it.",
        "Put your hand on the glass after. Still warm. They like it warm.",
        "My sister's boy took a lamp to the shed. Seven, he was. He'd wanted to see them."
      ],
      "notes": [
        { "title": "Lamp orders", "text": "No lamp to burn in an empty room or a window. The warden will put out any light found on its own, and the household fined." },
        { "title": "Said at the gate", "text": "Little lights don't come to the dark. They don't come to a train under way. Remember the first. The second's no comfort." }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "talkers", "creature": "gaunt",
      "law": "Somebody's always talking in {town}.",
      "hall": "the Reading Room", "hallStyle": "hall",
      "centrepiece": { "kind": "horn", "name": "the reading horn",
        "text": "A loudspeaker horn on a pole. A tired voice reads names, one after another. Every so often it reads the weather, then goes back to the names." },
      "lines": [
        "The horn reads the dead all night. Somebody has to. When it stops, we all start talking. Doesn't matter what.",
        "There's a thin one out by the old mill. Mrs. Gillis talks to it on her way to the well. She says it gets cross if she runs out of things to say.",
        "Don't go quiet on me. Tell me something. Anything. The price of flour.",
        "My husband stopped to cough. That was all. He stopped to cough.",
        "I talk in my sleep. The children say it's the only reason we're all still here.",
        "It doesn't want anything. It only wants company. Company's a terrible thing to want, out there.",
        "You're a quiet crowd. You'll want to fix that before the villages.",
        "Tell it a story. A long one. Don't stop at the good part."
      ],
      "notes": [
        { "title": "Readers wanted", "text": "The horn needs readers for the small hours. Any voice will do. Bring something long. Do not bring hymns." },
        { "title": "Scrawled", "text": "It was fine as long as Pell kept talking. Pell stopped to cough." }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "eyes", "creature": "sootChildren",
      "law": "Look them in the eye from five paces. Every one. Every time.",
      "hall": "the Glass House", "hallStyle": "hall",
      "centrepiece": { "kind": "mirror", "name": "the looking glass",
        "text": "A tall mirror in an iron frame, a white line painted five paces in front of it. The glass has been polished so often it's gone thin in the middle." },
      "lines": [
        "Stop there. On the line. Look at me. ... Go on, then.",
        "You'll hear children crying out past the wall. Some of them are children. That's what makes it so cruel.",
        "We took one in. Little girl, filthy, crying for her mam. The Moshers gave her their own bed.",
        "Look at the eyes. Then the hands. Don't let it get close enough to make you stop looking.",
        "We brought a real one in from {other}. She's the baker's now. She doesn't cry. Ever.",
        "Five paces. Not four. I paced it out myself, after the Moshers.",
        "If it calls you by name, it's heard someone say your name. Think who's been saying your name.",
        "A real one's worth more than your whole train. That's the trouble. That's always been the trouble."
      ],
      "notes": [
        { "title": "At the gate", "text": "All persons stop at the line and show their eyes. All persons. The gate warden has a mother too." },
        { "title": "Pinned to a door", "text": "She called me by my name. I never told her my name." }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "offerings", "creature": "trackDoll",
      "law": "Every toy in {town} goes on the shelf by the gate.",
      "hall": "the old school", "hallStyle": "school",
      "centrepiece": { "kind": "shelf", "name": "the toy shelf",
        "text": "A long shelf by the gate, worn pale where things stood. It's empty. There are small prints in the dust, and the prints don't go anywhere." },
      "lines": [
        "We give the toys to the line. She hasn't once come through the gate. Not once. Not yet.",
        "Ernie said he'd stop for her. He said it right up to the bridge.",
        "Our children don't have toys. They play with stones. They say they don't mind.",
        "She rode home in the cab of the Truro freight. They found the levers set just so. Nobody'd touched them.",
        "She likes the ones that make a noise. Music boxes. Drums. Anything you wind.",
        "Her face shines in the headlamp, they say. Like a plate. Like the moon lying on the rails.",
        "My Annie misses her rabbit. She asks after it. I tell her it's gone on a train.",
        "If she's aboard, give her something. Before she gets bored. You don't want her bored."
      ],
      "notes": [
        { "title": "Given this month", "text": "One tin drum. One rag rabbit (Annie's). One music box, wound. One spinning top. One china cup (refused, returned)." },
        { "title": "Driver's card", "text": "Found in the cab of the Truro freight, in the driver's hand: Doll on the line. Doll in the van. Doll in the" }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "rulebook", "creature": "whistler",
      "law": "Rule nine is read at every meal.",
      "hall": "the Rule Hall", "hallStyle": "hall",
      "centrepiece": { "kind": "lectern", "name": "the rule book",
        "text": "A railway rule book chained to a lectern, open at RULE 9. The rest of the page is torn out. Someone's written in the margin: ASK HARRY." },
      "lines": [
        "The whistle went with nobody in the cab. Harry went to look between the cars. We heard him calling for a day and a half.",
        "Rule nine. I can't say the rest. Nobody can. Harry knew it.",
        "It only happens when you're stood still. Moving, you're a train. Stood still, you're a pantry.",
        "If you go after them, go quick. It doesn't eat straight off. It takes you home first.",
        "We read a rule at every meal. The same one. You'd be surprised how much there is in it.",
        "Don't answer a whistle you didn't hear pulled. That's not the rule. That's just sense.",
        "The gaps between the cars are the worst place in the world. Dad says. Dad's never been between the cars.",
        "Harry's whistle sits on the lectern. Nobody's blown it. Nobody's going to."
      ],
      "notes": [
        { "title": "Tonight's rule", "text": "Rule 9. A whistle not blown by" },
        { "title": "In the margin", "text": "They take you somewhere first. Somebody could come for you, if they were quick about it." }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "banked", "creature": "stoker",
      "law": "No fire in {town} above the red mark.",
      "hall": "the Furnace Hall", "hallStyle": "hall",
      "centrepiece": { "kind": "brazier", "name": "the banked fire",
        "text": "An iron brazier banked to a sullen red. A poker lies across it, bent nearly double. Nobody's straightened it." },
      "lines": [
        "Run hot and you'll have company in your firebox. Ask anyone who lived on the east street. You can't.",
        "It comes up out of the coal. Glowing. Like a coal that's learned to crawl.",
        "Uncle Hector opened the door on it. Once. The poker's his.",
        "We'd sooner be cold. Everyone here's cold. You get used to the chilblains.",
        "The fire went green in the Moncton engine. The fireman wouldn't say what was in there. Wouldn't go near the tender after.",
        "Let it go out. That's what nobody wants to hear. Sometimes you let it go out.",
        "A hot engine's a fast engine. A fast engine's a lit window, to it.",
        "My hands haven't been warm since the fall. I don't miss it. I don't miss warm."
      ],
      "notes": [
        { "title": "Fire orders", "text": "All fires to be banked at dark. No fire above the red mark. Any fire found above the mark will be put out by the warden." },
        { "title": "Lost", "text": "Water hose, brass nozzle, from the Furnace Hall. Whoever has it: it's the only thing that worked." }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "crown", "creature": "draggers",
      "law": "Keep to the crown of the road.",
      "hall": "the Road House", "hallStyle": "hall",
      "centrepiece": { "kind": "line", "name": "the white line",
        "text": "A white line down the middle of the square, worn to the stone in the centre. Along the wall, a board with boots nailed to it. All left boots." },
      "lines": [
        "Edges are for falling off. Always were. Something helps, now.",
        "They found his boot on the roof of the van and the rest of him in the ditch a mile back.",
        "Walk the ridge on your roofs. Not the edge. Not to look over. Not for anything.",
        "If something takes your ankle, shout. Two seconds. Maybe three. That's what you've got.",
        "We built back from the road, after. Not for the light.",
        "Handrails, b'y. Whatever they cost you.",
        "I don't go near the edge of the platform. I don't go near the edge of my bed.",
        "It reaches further when you're going fast. Remember that. Or don't, and find out."
      ],
      "notes": [
        { "title": "Road orders", "text": "Walk the crown. Carts the crown. Children the crown. Anyone found at the edges after dark will be fined, if found." },
        { "title": "To the roofers", "text": "Ridge only. Ridge only. RIDGE ONLY." }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "backs", "creature": "followers",
      "law": "Brush down at every door.",
      "hall": "the Brushing House", "hallStyle": "hall",
      "centrepiece": { "kind": "pegs", "name": "the coat pegs",
        "text": "A row of pegs, every coat on them hung back to front, each brushed down the back so hard the cloth's gone thin." },
      "lines": [
        "Turn round. ... You're clear. Me? Go on. Properly. Harder than that.",
        "You can't see your own back. Nobody can. That's the whole of it.",
        "Gussie came home from the mill with something on her. We all saw it. She didn't. By the time we'd said...",
        "It sits where you can't look. It likes the best of what you've got. It went for the pantry first.",
        "My shoulders are raw. Everybody's shoulders here are raw.",
        "If somebody's looking past you at nothing, turn round. Turn round now.",
        "Don't trust a friend who won't look at your back. Don't trust one who looks too long, either.",
        "If your car smells of wet hay, don't put your hand in. Bring a light."
      ],
      "notes": [
        { "title": "At the gate", "text": "All persons coming in will be brushed down the back by the gate warden. All persons going out will brush each other." },
        { "title": "Nailed up", "text": "Rode in on Halloway: one. Rode in on Gillis: one. Rode in on the gate warden: nobody checked." }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "fours", "creature": "ribbits",
      "law": "Four out. Four back.",
      "hall": "the Four House", "hallStyle": "hall",
      "centrepiece": { "kind": "bench", "name": "the four bench",
        "text": "A bench long enough for four, four hooks over it and a lamp on each hook. One hook is empty. Nobody's hung a lamp on it since." },
      "lines": [
        "Two went out to the woodpile. One came back. She won't say. She hasn't said anything since.",
        "They don't bother anything as big as they are. That's what Dad said. Dad went out in a two.",
        "Four out, four back. Three's a meal, two's a snack, one's nothing at all. The children skip to it.",
        "Their tongues hold you still. Your friends can still move. That's what friends are for, now.",
        "They're slow if you run. Nobody runs. Everybody stands there thinking they'll be fine.",
        "We had a five once. Five was lovely. Nobody's got a five any more.",
        "Count them. Count you. If they've more, walk away together. Don't split up to go round.",
        "You can hear them in the reeds. Like frogs. Like big frogs. Like something laughing at frogs."
      ],
      "notes": [
        { "title": "Wall walk", "text": "Fours only. A three will be sent back. A two will not be let out." },
        { "title": "Lost", "text": "One boot, left, from a three that went out on the {other} road. No need to return it." }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "lastCar", "creature": "carHugger",
      "law": "Nothing rides in the last car.",
      "hall": "the Car Shed", "hallStyle": "shed",
      "centrepiece": { "kind": "carriage", "name": "the bitten van",
        "text": "The front half of an old van on blocks, the back half bitten off in one ragged curve. Inside, names are painted round the walls in white. The names go round to where the wall stops." },
      "lines": [
        "The Saint John freight came home short a van. Well. Not short. Eaten. You could see the teeth in the steel.",
        "Nothing in your last car you'd mind losing. Not cargo. Not people. Especially not people.",
        "You'll feel it before you hear it. The whole train goes heavy, like dragging a net.",
        "My brother was the brakeman. He rode the van. He always rode the van.",
        "You can cut it loose and watch it go off into the dark, still chewing. Or you can go back. Not alone.",
        "Stand in front of its mouth and you're freight.",
        "We painted their names in what's left of the van. Ran out of wall.",
        "Three of you can kill it quick, they say. They say a lot of things."
      ],
      "notes": [
        { "title": "Loading orders", "text": "Nothing of value in the last car. The last car is for empties, ballast and bad apples." },
        { "title": "From the clerk", "text": "The clerk does not pay out on last cars. Read your contract." }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "backGun", "creature": "cinderHounds",
      "law": "The back gun is never left alone.",
      "hall": "the Gun House", "hallStyle": "hall",
      "centrepiece": { "kind": "cannon", "name": "the old gun",
        "text": "A short iron cannon on a timber carriage, aimed back up the line. Its brass ring is polished every night. Under it, a tin of matches and a list of names." },
      "lines": [
        "They run behind. Always behind. Gaining on every hill.",
        "The Pictou freight was slow on the grade. They came over the back. We heard the crew on the radio till the radio stopped.",
        "Dad kept the back gun loaded and he came home every time. Every time but one.",
        "Once they're on, they don't get off. They eat what you're hauling and set it going.",
        "Livestock draws them. A hot boiler too. You've got both. God love you.",
        "You'll hear paws on the iron. Then you'll smell smoke. That's them, not you.",
        "We fire the old gun at the hour, every night, for the Pictou crew. The noise is for them.",
        "Loud, the gun. Loud's the price. Everything out there has a price."
      ],
      "notes": [
        { "title": "Gun drill", "text": "Back gun loaded before the throat. Back gun crewed past the last light. No exceptions for weather, tiredness or cards." },
        { "title": "Lost", "text": "One dog. Brown. Not ours. Not a dog." }
      ]
    }
  ],

  // A town's smaller habits, one or two a town, on top of its custom. None is a creature's rule; most are grief.
  "quirks": [
    { "id": "names", "habit": "children aren't named till their first winter",
      "lines": ["My name? I've not had it long. Got it when I was nine. We wait, here.", "Don't name a thing till it's lasted a winter. Goes for trains too."],
      "notes": [{ "title": "Named this winter", "text": "Ash. Ivo. The Gillis boy. The girl from the east street. The goat." }] },
    { "id": "coats", "habit": "they wear their dead's coats",
      "lines": ["This was my brother's coat. Fits me better than it did him, he'd say.", "Every coat in {town} was somebody's. Mine was three people's."],
      "notes": [{ "title": "Coats", "text": "Coats of the departed to be brought to the hall for fitting. Pockets to be emptied first. Mostly it's notes." }] },
    { "id": "midnight", "habit": "the whole town eats together at midnight",
      "lines": ["Supper's at midnight. Everyone. That way we know who's missing before we start.", "You'll miss supper. Shame. There's a place laid for a crew. There always is."],
      "notes": [{ "title": "Supper", "text": "Midnight, the long table. Do not sit at a place that isn't yours, even if it's empty. Especially then." }] },
    { "id": "goats", "habit": "they keep goats on the roofs",
      "lines": ["Goats on the roofs. Up there they see things coming, and they make a fuss.", "Don't laugh at the goats. The goats have saved this town more times than the guns."],
      "notes": [{ "title": "Goat watch", "text": "Any goat bleating after dark is to be reported to the wall. Any goat not bleating after dark is also to be reported." }] },
    { "id": "redDoors", "habit": "every door is painted red",
      "lines": ["Red doors. So you can find yours in the dark. Or so something can't. Depends who you ask.", "Paint's the one thing we've never run short of. Funny, that."],
      "notes": [{ "title": "Paint", "text": "Doors to be repainted red by the end of the month. Scrape the old first. Don't look too close at what's under it." }] },
    { "id": "clocks", "habit": "every clock in town stopped at the same minute",
      "lines": ["Ten past four. Every clock in {town}. We leave them be. It'd feel rude to wind them.", "We tell the time by the trains. Yours is the last tonight."],
      "notes": [{ "title": "Clock repairs", "text": "The clockmaker is no longer taking work. The clockmaker asks that you stop bringing him the clocks." }] },
    { "id": "foghorn", "habit": "the foghorn at the point still sounds, though nobody's at the point",
      "lines": ["Hear that? The horn at the point. Nobody's been out to the point since the fall.", "It goes when the fog's in. It goes when it's not, too. Lately."],
      "notes": [{ "title": "Lighthouse", "text": "Volunteers wanted for the point. To see who's sounding the horn. Volunteers wanted. Volunteers wanted." }] },
    { "id": "owing", "habit": "everyone owes everyone",
      "lines": ["You owe me for that look. Everyone owes everyone here. Keeps people coming back.", "My father died owing {name} a shovel. We're still paying it off."],
      "notes": [{ "title": "Owed", "text": "To {name}: one shovel, one lamp glass, one apology. Interest as agreed." }] },
    { "id": "gateWeddings", "habit": "they marry at the gate, so a crew can witness",
      "lines": ["We marry at the gate, so the train crews can witness. You'd do. Got a tie?", "Crews are lucky at a wedding. Unless they're the last crew out. Then they're very unlucky."],
      "notes": [{ "title": "Banns", "text": "{name} and {name2} will be married at the gate when the next crew is in. Objections to the clerk. Witnesses wanted." }] },
    { "id": "salt", "habit": "salt on every sill",
      "lines": ["Salt on the sill. Doesn't stop anything. Makes us feel better.", "We traded the last of the fish for salt. Couldn't tell you why. Everyone did it at once."],
      "notes": [{ "title": "Salt", "text": "One cup per sill per week. Any more is waste. Any less is your own lookout." }] },
    { "id": "lastWords", "habit": "they write down everyone's last words",
      "lines": ["We write down everyone's last words. Most of them are 'what was that'.", "The book of last words is in the hall. You're welcome to read it. Don't add to it."],
      "notes": [{ "title": "From the book of last words", "text": "'Who left this here?' 'I'll only be a minute.' 'It's fine, it's one of ours.' 'Shh.'" }] }
  ],

  // What the town makes (linegen tiers.json fortress "identities"; GDD §3: "one settlement produces coal ...").
  "industries": {
    "coal": { "hand": "pit hand", "name": "a pit town",
      "stalls": ["Lamp wicks, a tray of pit tallies with names stamped on them, one canary cage with the door open.",
        "Boots, mostly left ones. A card: RIGHT BOOTS FROM THE PIT, ASK AT THE HEADFRAME."],
      "lines": ["Night shift comes up at first light. We don't ask what they heard down there. They tell us anyway.",
        "Every lump of coal in your tender came up through that headframe. You're welcome.",
        "The seam runs out under the wall. We follow it as far as the lamps reach. Then we stop. Something else is following it the other way."],
      "notes": [{ "title": "Pit notice", "text": "Shift change at the bell. Lamps checked out and checked in. A lamp not checked in is a man not checked in." },
        { "title": "Tonnage", "text": "This week: {n}00 tons up. {n} props lost. Seam C closed until further notice. Do not ask about seam C." }] },
    "farm": { "hand": "grower", "name": "a growing town",
      "stalls": ["Turnips, potatoes, the odd onion under a sack. Nobody minding it. Nobody needs to.",
        "Seed packets tied in string, each labelled with the bed it came from and the year it last came up."],
      "lines": ["Grow lamps on every bed. The plants don't know it's dark. We don't tell them.",
        "The cattle are under the wall. Safest cows in the territory. They sleep better than me.",
        "Something's been in the potatoes. Not eating them. Just in them. Moving them about."],
      "notes": [{ "title": "Grow-lamp rota", "text": "Beds to be lit in turn. No bed to go dark for more than an hour. A bed gone dark is a bed lost." },
        { "title": "Market", "text": "Turnips, potatoes, the odd onion. Meat Thursdays. Do not ask what the pigs eat." }] },
    "foundry": { "hand": "foundry hand", "name": "a foundry town",
      "stalls": ["Nails, hinges, a box of cast iron saints. The saints are all turned to face the wall.",
        "Pans, kettles, a stove door. Everything still warm to the touch."],
      "lines": ["The furnaces never go out. You'll see the glow over the wall for miles. So will everything else.",
        "Castings for half the line come out of here. Heavy as sin. Mind your feet under the crane.",
        "My hands haven't been cold in eleven years. Nothing else about me's been warm."],
      "notes": [{ "title": "Pour schedule", "text": "Pours at the bell. Nobody under the gantry while the ladle's up. Nobody. That means you, {name}." },
        { "title": "Wanted at the foundry", "text": "Strikers, pourers, a crane hand who can see the ground. Pay in scrip, coal and soup." }] }
  },

  // The jobs people do about the square, each with what the job makes them say.
  "roles": {
    "gatekeeper": { "title": "gatekeeper", "lines": [
      "Gate opens when your wheels turn. Not before. Not for anyone. Not for anyone who asks nicely, either.",
      "I count them out and I count them in. The numbers don't have to match. They usually do. Usually.",
      "Last crew out of this gate didn't come back. Paid up front, though. Lovely people.",
      "Sign the book going out. Sign it coming back. We compare the hands.",
      "Six inches of oak and iron, that gate. It doesn't keep it out. It keeps it out long enough." ] },
    "guard": { "title": "wall guard", "lines": [
      "We shoot what comes to the wall. Nothing comes to the wall. We still shoot.",
      "From up there you can see your train all the way to the first bend. Then you're the dark's.",
      "There's a lamp out past the last tower nobody lit. Been there three nights. We don't go and look.",
      "You learn to tell a lamp from an eye at four hundred yards. Mostly. Mostly's a long word, up there.",
      "Some nights something walks the outside of the wall all the way round. Never tries the gate. Just walks." ] },
    "clerk": { "title": "clerk", "lines": [
      "Freight's paid at the far gate, not this one. Bodies at seventy-five. I don't set the rates. I'd set them lower.",
      "Sign here. And here. That one's your next of kin. Leave it blank if it's each other.",
      "A car's a car whether it's full of coal or full of holes. We pay by what's in it, mind.",
      "We don't pay a crewman's wages in cash. We take a forwarding address. You'd be surprised how many have none.",
      "One ledger for the crews that came back, one for the rest. I'll want a new one of the second soon." ] },
    "keeper": { "title": "keeper of {hall}", "lines": [
      "You'll have heard what we do here. We do it because of what happened. That's all I'll say.",
      "We keep {hall} because somebody has to. And because the last keeper can't.",
      "Every custom in this town is a grave somebody didn't need.",
      "A superstition's what you do when you're not sure. We're sure.",
      "We didn't make it up. We copied it off the ones who lived." ] },
    "storekeeper": { "title": "storekeeper", "lines": [
      "Lamps, extinguishers, powder. Everything's dear. You're welcome to go and fetch it yourself.",
      "Spare engineering kit, little used. Previous owner won't be needing it.",
      "I'd sell you luck if I had any. Sold the last of it to the crew before you.",
      "Matches, plenty. Lamp oil, some. Powder, ask me tomorrow.",
      "Everything in here's been on a train once. Most of it twice. Some of it came back on its own." ] },
    "fitter": { "title": "fitter", "lines": [
      "Your boiler's sound. Sound as anything round here. Don't let it sit at the red.",
      "The engineering kit lives in the fitter's locker. If it's not there, it's somewhere worse.",
      "Lose the kit and burst the boiler and you're walking home. Nobody walks home.",
      "Listen to her cold, before you light up. A sound engine ticks. A wrong one whispers.",
      "Patched a boiler once with a dead man's plate. Held fine. Never ran the same." ] },
    "lampman": { "title": "lampman", "lines": [
      "Every lamp on your train I filled myself. If one goes out, it wasn't the oil.",
      "Smashed lamps I can fix. Smashed lampmen, less so.",
      "Keep a spare lamp in a locker. Keep a spare lampman too, if you can find one.",
      "A lamp in a window's a lamp for anything that's looking. Shade yours on the bends.",
      "Lamps don't go out on their own out there. Something has to want them out." ] },
    "porter": { "title": "porter", "lines": [
      "I carry what the crews leave behind. You'd be surprised how much of it is people.",
      "Mind the gap. That's a joke from before. It isn't one now.",
      "Something in your third car's knocking. Probably the load. Probably.",
      "Trunks come back without owners all the time. Had an owner come back without a trunk. That was worse.",
      "I don't open what's left behind. I weigh it. Some of it weighs different in the morning." ] },
    "driver": { "title": "old driver", "lines": [
      "Drove the {other} road nineteen years. Never stopped for anything on the line. That's the whole trick.",
      "Watch the bends on your map, not the bends in front of you. In front of you is too late.",
      "Driver's the one they blame. Driver's the one who's always in the cab. Think on that.",
      "On the long flats you'll want to open her right up. Everybody does. That's what the flats are for.",
      "If the rails hum while you're stood, get her moving. Don't wait to see what's coming down them." ] },
    "widow": { "title": "widow", "lines": [
      "My husband drove the night mail. They paid me seventy-five. I keep wondering what the other twenty-five was.",
      "Bring them home if you can. Even if it's only the coat.",
      "I meet every train. One night it'll be his.",
      "I still set his place. The plate's always moved by morning. Just a little. Like somebody nudged it.",
      "They gave me his cap back. Not his coat, not his boots. Just the cap. Dry, on a wet night." ] },
    "cook": { "title": "cook", "lines": [
      "Eat now. Nobody eats out there. Well. Nobody you'd want to watch eating.",
      "Chowder. It's always chowder. Don't ask what's in it. Don't ask what isn't.",
      "Crews get double. Not because you work harder. Because it's the last meal for some of you.",
      "Take bread. Bread keeps. Everything else out there goes off, or goes on without you.",
      "I cook for the number on the board. Lately they tell me cook for one more. Nobody'll say who for." ] },
    // Not a job: what anyone in any town might say, when the rest has been said.
    "anyone": { "title": "townsman", "lines": [
      "Some cold tonight. It'll be colder out there. It's always colder out there.",
      "First night out? You've the look. It wears off, one way or another.",
      "I had a cousin on the trains. Had.",
      "Bring us something back. Anything. We're not fussy any more.",
      "Don't stand there gawking. You'll miss your dawn.",
      "The quiet crews come back more often. Couldn't tell you why.",
      "Did you hear that? No? Good. Good.",
      "If you see my brother out there, tell him the goat died. He'll want to know.",
      "I've never been past the gate. They tell me it's much the same, only worse.",
      "Your lamps look low. Everybody's lamps look low. It's the dark, not the oil.",
      "In {other} they won't sing. Not at a wedding, not at a burying. I thought that was daft till I heard what sings back.",
      "They say in {other} nobody's ever left stood on their own. I tried it once, out back. I didn't like what the quiet did.",
      "Folk in {other} count each other at supper, out loud. One night the count came out one over. They kept counting.",
      "In {other} they keep the windows black. A light in a window's an invitation, they say. To what, they don't say.",
      "They keep somebody talking all night in {other}, in shifts, like a fire. I asked what happens if it goes out. Nobody laughed.",
      "Gran said look a strange child in the eye or don't look at all. She never said why. She never had to.",
      "In {other} the children leave their toys at the gate. Polite, they call it. Polite to who?",
      "There's a book of rules in {other}, read out at meals. They won't read you rule nine unless you sign on.",
      "Keep your fire low and your door shut. A greedy fire draws company.",
      "Keep to the middle of things. The road, the roof, the room. The edges are where you get took.",
      "Brush your coat down before you go in a house. Every house. Even your own. Especially your own.",
      "Go out four, come back four. If you're three, think hard about coming back at all.",
      "Never ride the last car. Uncle did. The train came in a car short and nobody heard it go.",
      "Somebody on the back gun. Always. Even in the yard. Especially in the yard.",
      "Never whistle walking home. Not a tune, not a note. Whistling's for trains.",
      "There was a dance at the hall, before. Fiddles and all. Nobody's danced since the fiddle kept on after the fiddler stopped.",
      "Fog's in. Fog's always in now. When it lifts we count the houses.",
      "My boy draws trains. Every one the same: long and black, no lamps, and a face at every window.",
      "We buried Dad with his boots on. In case. Don't look at me like that, everybody does it now.",
      "The sea used to come up to the station. Now it comes up to the wall. Some nights it knocks.",
      "I keep his dory painted. He'll not need it. I keep it painted.",
      "They rang the bell for the lost crews till the rope wore through. Then we stopped. Then it rang itself.",
      "Traps came up full of hair one spring. Nobody's set a trap since.",
      "If you hear your name from the trees, it isn't your mother. Your mother's here. She's right here.",
      "We were three hundred, once. You can tell by the chairs.",
      "I was on the platform the night the mail came in empty. Doors open, lamps lit, kettle on in the van. Still warm.",
      "Bring back anything with writing on it. A ticket. A tag. We're trying to work out who's left.",
      "Some days I think the wall's to keep us in. Then I remember the nights before the wall.",
      "You smell that? Salt and hot iron. That's the line breathing. It does that, nights.",
      "The minister went on the first train after. Said he'd be back with help. Eleven years, that was.",
      "I've a sister in {other}. Had a letter in the spring. Her handwriting. Not her words.",
      "Mam says the stars moved after the fall. Not much. Enough to lose your way by.",
      "Don't count the cars going round the bend. You'll get a different number coming back.",
      "The children play trains in the square. Somebody's the engine, somebody's the crew. Nobody'll be the last car.",
      "Every house here has a chair nobody sits in. Don't ask whose. Just don't sit.",
      "The wireless picks up a voice some nights, reading names. Ours, mostly. In order.",
      "I don't sleep. Haven't since. I sit at the window and count the lamps on the wall." ] }
  },

  // A household behind an open door (an explorable house): what's happened to it, what each of them says about it, and
  // the thing in the house that tells it. {family} is their surname, {absent} the one they lost.
  "households": [
    { "id": "emptyChair", "absent": true,
      "lines": {
        "elder": ["{absent} sat there. Still does, as far as I'm concerned. Don't you sit there.", "We lay for {absent} every night. One night the plate'll be empty in the morning."],
        "parent": ["{absent} went out on the wall walk and the walk came back short. That's how they said it. The walk came back short.", "You'll stay for tea? No. No, you can't. That's not your chair."],
        "child": ["{absent}'s not dead. Mam says. {absent}'s on a train.", "I set the table. I always set it right."] },
      "object": { "kind": "table", "name": "a place laid", "text": "A place laid at the end of the table: a plate, a cup, a fork. The cup's been filled. It's cold. There's a ring under it from all the other nights." } },
    { "id": "sleepwalker", "absent": false,
      "lines": {
        "parent": ["She walks to the gate in her sleep. Every night. We tied a bell to her ankle. Now we hear her go.", "She says someone calls her from the other side of the wall. In my voice."],
        "child": ["I don't walk anywhere. I'm asleep the whole time. Ask anyone.", "There's a lady by the gate who sings to me. Don't tell Mam she sings."],
        "elder": ["Let the child sleep. Let her sleep. Don't wake her. Never wake her when she's walking."] },
      "object": { "kind": "anklebell", "name": "a bell on a string", "text": "A little brass bell on a length of string, tied to the bedpost. The string's been cut through and knotted back together, many times." } },
    { "id": "window", "absent": true,
      "lines": {
        "elder": ["The 41's due. Due any minute. I'll just watch a while longer.", "{absent} drove the 41. You'll have passed it on the line? A big black engine, with a red stripe? No?"],
        "parent": ["She's sat at that window eleven years. We stopped telling her. It's kinder.", "Some nights I look out with her. Some nights I almost see it."],
        "child": ["Gran says there's a train coming. There's always a train coming, with Gran."] },
      "object": { "kind": "timetable", "name": "a timetable", "text": "A railway timetable from before, framed. One train is ringed in pencil: No. 41, the night mail, due 3:10. The ring's been gone over so often the paper's through." } },
    { "id": "cameBack", "absent": false,
      "lines": {
        "parent": ["He came back from the run on his own. Walked in at dawn. He's been very good since. Very quiet. He doesn't sleep.", "He stands at the foot of the bed. All night. Just stands. I tell myself he's keeping watch."],
        "elder": ["That's not how he used to hold his cup.", "Ask him where he left the rest of the crew. Go on. I've asked. Ask him."],
        "lodger": ["Lovely man, the mister. Never tired. Never hungry. Never anything."] },
      "object": { "kind": "boots", "name": "a pair of boots", "text": "A pair of railwayman's boots by the door, laced and polished. They've never been worn. His others are still on him." } },
    { "id": "boards", "absent": false,
      "lines": {
        "parent": ["I board the windows every night. Every night. They're boarded already. I do it again.", "Hear that? Like a nail drawn out. That's not me. That's never me."],
        "child": ["Dad hammers all night. I count the nails. I'm up to a hundred and nine.", "One of the boards was on the outside this morning. Dad says I imagined it."],
        "elder": ["He's not mad. He's the only sane one of us. Pass me that hammer."] },
      "object": { "kind": "boards", "name": "the boarded window", "text": "Boards nailed over the window from inside, fresh nails driven in among old ones. Between two boards, from the outside, something has scratched a very small hole." } },
    { "id": "lodgers", "absent": false,
      "lines": {
        "parent": ["They came in from {other} after it went quiet. We had the room. They're very grateful. They watch us sleep.", "They don't talk about {other}. When the children ask, they smile."],
        "lodger": ["{other}? A good town. Quiet now. We're lucky to be here. We're lucky.", "Your train. How many does it seat? Only asking. Only asking."],
        "child": ["The lodgers eat after we've gone to bed. I hear them. They eat a lot."] },
      "object": { "kind": "suitcase", "name": "the lodgers' case", "text": "A cardboard suitcase under the stairs, strapped shut. A label: {other}. Under it another label, older, from a town that isn't on any map you've got." } },
    { "id": "letters", "absent": true,
      "lines": {
        "parent": ["I write to {absent} every week. Care of Dispatch. Dispatch knows where every train is.", "He'll answer when the line's clear. The line's never clear."],
        "elder": ["Let her write. The day she stops, I'll worry."],
        "child": ["I wrote to {absent} too. I drew the house. I drew us all in the window so he'd know which one."] },
      "object": { "kind": "letters", "name": "a stack of letters", "text": "Letters tied in string, every one addressed to {absent}, care of the night mail, care of Dispatch. All stamped. None sent. The top one just says COME HOME, over and over, down the page." } },
    { "id": "cradle", "absent": false,
      "lines": {
        "parent": ["She was born after the fall. She's never seen daylight. She doesn't miss it. She doesn't cry.", "Rock her if you like. Don't look in for long. She doesn't like to be looked at long."],
        "elder": ["Quietest baby I ever saw. Never once cried. Never once."],
        "child": ["My sister's very good. Mam says so. She just lies there and listens."] },
      "object": { "kind": "cradle", "name": "the cradle", "text": "A wooden cradle, rocked so often there are two worn lines in the floor under it. The blanket's drawn right up. Something under it is breathing very slowly." } },
    { "id": "radio", "absent": true,
      "lines": {
        "elder": ["The wireless picks up names some nights. Ours, once. We all sat very still till it moved on.", "{absent} used to listen to the hockey on that. From the mainland. Imagine. Hockey."],
        "parent": ["Leave it off. No. Leave it on. If it's on, we hear it coming.", "It counts, sometimes. Under the static. It got to eleven last night."],
        "child": ["The radio said my name. I said hello back. Was that wrong?"] },
      "object": { "kind": "radio", "name": "the wireless", "text": "A wooden cabinet radio, warm, hissing. Under the static, very faint, someone is counting. They've just passed your number." } },
    { "id": "clock", "absent": true,
      "lines": {
        "elder": ["Ten past four. The minute {absent} went. I stopped it myself. Don't you touch it.", "Some nights the clock ticks. Just a few. Then it thinks better of it."],
        "parent": ["Every clock in the house stopped together. All of them. We hadn't even heard yet."],
        "child": ["I wound the clock once. Mam cried and cried. I won't again."] },
      "object": { "kind": "clock", "name": "the mantel clock", "text": "A mantel clock stopped at ten past four. The key's gone. Somebody's tied the pendulum still with a hair ribbon." } },
    { "id": "foghorn", "absent": true,
      "lines": {
        "parent": ["{absent} took the dory out the night of the fall. The fog came in behind him and never went back out.", "We sound the horn for him at the turn of every tide. Some nights something out there sounds back."],
        "elder": ["Forty years I fished. You don't whistle on a boat. You don't whistle anywhere, now. Nobody told {absent}.", "The fog's not weather any more. It comes in when it's called."],
        "child": ["Dad's out past the light. He waves from the fog some mornings. Mam won't look.", "I'm learning the horn. Two long, one short. That's ours. Anything else, you don't answer."] },
      "object": { "kind": "timetable", "name": "a tide table", "text": "A tide table from the year of the fall, framed on the wall. One high water is ringed in pencil: the night the dories went out. Every tide since is ticked off, in the same hand, to tonight's." } },
    { "id": "rope", "absent": false,
      "lines": {
        "parent": ["Nobody in this house goes to the privy alone. Not the children. Not me. Especially not me.", "My brother went for wood by himself. Two minutes. We heard him counting all the way back. It wasn't him counting."],
        "child": ["I hold Mam's hand on the stairs. If I let go it's my fault.", "Someone stood right behind me at the pump. I didn't turn round. You don't turn round. You wait for your other."],
        "elder": ["Mind the rope. A rope's better than a hand. A hand lets go when it's frightened."] },
      "object": { "kind": "boots", "name": "a pair of boots", "text": "A pair of boots by the door with a rope knotted through both, long enough to reach a second pair. The second pair's gone. The rope's end has been cut, very cleanly." } },
    { "id": "toys", "absent": true,
      "lines": {
        "parent": ["We don't keep toys in this house. Not since {absent}. Whatever comes, we give it to the shelf at the gate.", "She's polite, the one who takes them. Polite ones you thank. You thank them and you shut the door."],
        "child": ["I had a rag doll. Somebody wanted her more than me. That's what Mam says. Somebody wanted her more.", "If you find a toy on the line, don't pick it up. It's not yours. It's not anybody's yet."],
        "elder": ["In my day a doll was a doll. Now you don't leave one lying. Not on a sill. Not anywhere it looks like a gift."] },
      "object": { "kind": "suitcase", "name": "a child's case", "text": "A child's case under the stairs, strapped shut. Through a split in its side: a doll's arm, a tin wheel. A luggage tag in a child's hand: FOR HER. SHE ASKED NICELY." } },
    { "id": "pit", "absent": true,
      "lines": {
        "parent": ["{absent} went down the pit the morning after the fall. The shift came up at four. He came up at six. He's been down since.", "The company sent his lamp back. Lit. It's never needed filling."],
        "elder": ["Fifty years under the sea in the deep seam. Never heard singing down there till after. You never sing back.", "There's coal in the walls of every house in this town. Some nights you hear it settle. Some nights it's not settling."],
        "child": ["There's black footprints by Dad's boots most mornings. Mam sweeps them before Gran sees."] },
      "object": { "kind": "boots", "name": "a miner's boots", "text": "A miner's boots by the door, the toes worn white. In front of them, on the swept floor, fresh coal dust in the shape of two feet, standing very still, facing in." } },
    { "id": "ceilidh", "absent": true,
      "lines": {
        "parent": ["We were a singing house. A kitchen party every Saturday. {absent} had the best voice on the shore. That was the trouble.", "Somebody sang at the last one. Just a verse. We counted heads in the morning."],
        "elder": ["Hum if you must. Mouth shut, under your breath. Never the words. The words carry.", "The fiddle's in its case. It stays in its case. The bow's under my mattress."],
        "child": ["Gran taught me a song with no tune. You just say it. Like a list of who's still here."] },
      "object": { "kind": "letters", "name": "folded sheet music", "text": "Sheet music, folded small and tied with a fiddle string. Every tune's still there. Every word has been scratched out, so hard the nib went through." } },
    { "id": "count", "absent": false,
      "lines": {
        "parent": ["I count us every hour. Five of us. Every hour, five. One night there were six, and nobody would say who.", "Count anything that comes in a crowd. If they're more than you, you're already too late to be clever."],
        "elder": ["She counts. Let her count. Somebody in every house should."],
        "child": ["Mam counts us when we're asleep. I pretend, so she gets it right.", "I counted the frogs in the ditch once. I stopped. They didn't."] },
      "object": { "kind": "letters", "name": "a tally", "text": "Envelopes, opened flat, covered in tally marks in pencil: the household counted every hour, every night. Five. Five. Five. Six. Five. Five. The six has been rubbed at until the paper's thin." } },
    { "id": "telephone", "absent": true,
      "lines": {
        "parent": ["{absent} was a brakeman. The night the line went dark he rang us on the railway telephone. He still rings. They cut that wire years ago.", "He asks for the door to be opened. Only that. I tell him it's open. It's not. It never will be."],
        "elder": ["When it whispers you turn it up. When it whistles you turn it off and leave the room. I don't ask why any more."],
        "child": ["The radio has Dad's voice in it some nights. He knows all our names. He says them in the wrong order."] },
      "object": { "kind": "radio", "name": "the wireless", "text": "A cabinet wireless with its dial taped down between two stations. On the tape, in pencil: HE'S ON THIS ONE. It isn't switched on. It's warm." } },
    { "id": "stones", "absent": false,
      "lines": {
        "parent": ["A stone on the sill for every night we've all slept in. The row's gone round the room twice. Don't move any.", "If you hear tapping on the glass, it's the stones settling. That's what we say. That's what we've decided."],
        "child": ["I picked the stones off the beach. Only white ones. Anything dark that washes up, you leave.", "One morning there was a stone on the outside sill too. Dad buried it."],
        "lodger": ["They're good people. Strange about the stones. I've started counting them myself. I can't stop."] },
      "object": { "kind": "boards", "name": "the boarded window", "text": "Boards nailed across the window, and along the sill inside them a row of small white beach stones, one for every night. The row runs off the sill and on round the room." } },
    { "id": "bride", "absent": true,
      "lines": {
        "parent": ["{absent} was to be married the week of the fall. The dress is upstairs. Some nights it isn't.", "We set her place. It's what you do. It's what the town does. Don't take her cup."],
        "elder": ["She was always one for the platform. Waving the trains off. Waving them in. You don't wave at a train now. It might wave back."],
        "child": ["My sister comes home late. She doesn't use the door. She's very quiet so she doesn't wake Mam."] },
      "object": { "kind": "table", "name": "a place laid", "text": "A place laid at the table with the good china: a wedding plate, gold at the rim, and a cup. The cup's half full. There's a lip print on it, very faint, the colour of river mud." } },
    { "id": "lighthouse", "absent": true,
      "lines": {
        "parent": ["{absent} kept the light on the point. It's still turning. Nobody's been out to it in nine years.", "A lit window tells something where you are. We keep ours dark. Keep yours dark, out there."],
        "elder": ["The light used to warn the ships off the rocks. Now we wonder who it's calling in."],
        "child": ["I watch the light from my window. Three flashes, then dark. Last night it was four."] },
      "object": { "kind": "clock", "name": "the keeper's clock", "text": "A brass-faced keeper's clock on the mantel, still wound, still keeping time. Beside it, the logbook's last line, in a different hand: LIGHT ATTENDED. ALL WELL. KEEPER GONE DOWN TO THE WATER." } }
  ],

  // A knock at a shut house, one a house.
  "houseKnocks": [
    "Nobody answers. The lamp inside goes out.",
    "A child's voice, through the door: Mam says we're not in.",
    "Footsteps come to the door and stop. They don't go away.",
    "Who is it? You tell them. A long pause. No it isn't.",
    "Somebody inside is counting. Your knock makes them lose their place. They start again from one.",
    "The door opens an inch on a chain. An eye. The door shuts.",
    "Go away, crew. Go away, go away, and come back with him.",
    "Through the door, very close, someone is breathing. They're waiting for you to knock again.",
    "A dog barks once inside and is stopped.",
    "Laughter, a whole family's, then silence, all at once, as if somebody had shushed them."
  ],
  // A house nobody lives in now, looked at: shut, boarded or burnt.
  "emptyHouses": {
    "boarded": ["Nailed shut from the outside. A name and a date in whitewash on the door.", "The windows are boarded from inside. Somebody was still in there when they did it.",
      "Boarded up, a cross painted on the boards. Under the cross, smaller: SORRY.", "Shut and boarded. On the step, a child's shoe, left out as if for somebody to come back for it."],
    "burnt": ["Burnt to the sills. The chimney's standing, and somebody's hung a wreath on it.", "Burnt out. The stove's still there in the ashes, its door open. Inside the stove, a doll's head."],
    "open": ["Empty. The door's open. Inside, the table's laid for five.", "Empty, the door off its hinges. On every wall, the same word, small, in pencil: HERE."]
  },

  // Threads that run through the whole world, a scrap or two in each town (App. F.1: "some interactions give a scrap of
  // story; most is left to inference"). Nothing here is ever explained.
  "threads": [
    { "id": "fall",
      "lines": ["Gran saw it come down. She said the sky rang like a struck rail for a week, and the sea went the colour of tea.",
        "Comet glass hums if you hold it. Don't hold it near your teeth.", "It came down in the bay. The fishermen say the water's warm there still. Nobody fishes it." ],
      "notes": [{ "title": "Notice", "text": "Comet-derived material is not to be stored inside the walls. It is to be shipped. It is always to be shipped." }] },
    { "id": "lostTrains",
      "lines": ["The night mail left {other} with nine aboard. Dispatch heard it on the radio three nights after. Just the engine, breathing.",
        "There's a crew on the board at the station that's been due eleven years. We still leave a lamp in for them.", "Number nine came into {other} a day late with the brakes on and every lamp lit. Nobody aboard. Dinner not touched." ],
      "notes": [{ "title": "Overdue", "text": "No. 41, the night mail. Nine crew. Due at {town} on the fourth. Still due." }] },
    { "id": "deadLines",
      "lines": ["{other} stopped answering in the spring. The line's still open. Somebody still throws the switches out there.",
        "When a town goes quiet, the lights stay on. That's the bit nobody likes.", "You can still ring {other} on the telephone. Somebody picks up. They just breathe, and listen." ],
      "notes": [{ "title": "From the radio log", "text": "{other}, 02:10: all well. 02:40: all well. 03:10: all well. 03:40: all well, all well, all well." }] },
    { "id": "sea",
      "lines": ["The boats stopped going out the first winter. The ones out came back on their own. Nobody aboard. Nets full.",
        "Don't look at the water from the wharf at night. Something looks back, and it's patient.", "The tide comes in twice a day and once at night when it shouldn't. You'll know that one when you hear it." ],
      "notes": [{ "title": "Harbour notice", "text": "The wharf is closed until further notice. Anyone hearing the ferry horn is to report it to the council. There is no ferry." }] },
    { "id": "penal",
      "lines": ["They sent the convicts out to work the far yards. Forty men and two guards a car. It was the guards didn't come back.",
        "If you find a man in greys out there, he's probably fine. Probably.", "The penal cars ran Saturdays, barred windows and all. They still run Saturdays, they say. Empty. Barred." ],
      "notes": [{ "title": "Wanted", "text": "Penal labour for the {other} yards. Free passage out. Passage back by arrangement." }] },
    { "id": "dispatch",
      "lines": ["Nobody's met Dispatch. Dispatch has never once been wrong about the line.",
        "Dawn opens the main line. Nobody here's seen the day trains. You only hear them.", "Dispatch knows. Dispatch always knows. That's the trouble. It knows, and it sends you anyway." ],
      "notes": [{ "title": "From Dispatch", "text": "Night freight is to clear the main line before first light. Dispatch accepts no responsibility for any train on the main line after first light." }] },
    { "id": "fees",
      "lines": ["The clerk pays seventy-five for a body brought home. It used to be eighty. Nobody asked why.",
        "If you see a lamp in a halt, someone's in there waiting for a train. Could be yours.", "Seventy-five for a body. They take the coat off first. A good coat's worth more." ],
      "notes": [{ "title": "Schedule of fees", "text": "Crew lost: the full fee. Crew lost, body brought home: a quarter of the fee. Crew lost, body brought home, still talking: see the clerk." }] }
  ],

  // Notices any town's board might carry.
  "notices": [
    { "title": "Rations", "text": "Bread: half. Coal: half. Lamp oil: as before. Complaints to the clerk, in writing." },
    { "title": "Crews wanted", "text": "Freight crews wanted for the {other} road. Must climb, carry and keep calm. Paid on delivery. Fees on non-delivery." },
    { "title": "Lost and found", "text": "Found: one left glove, one lamp glass, one name tag (Corkum). Lost: Corkum." },
    { "title": "From the wall", "text": "Lamps sighted past the last tower: {n}. Lamps we lit past the last tower: none." }
  ],

  // A knock at one of the square's doors: the custom's own building, the clerk's office, the stores. One a door.
  "doors": {
    "council": ["Nobody answers. Through the glass, a long table, a chair at its end pulled out, papers squared at every place but that one.",
      "A clerk's voice through the slot: the council sits Thursdays, write it down and put it through. The slot has been taped over from inside."],
    "quiet": ["Something moves inside, then stops. Whoever it is doesn't come to the door. On the step, the bowl is full.",
      "Nobody answers. The bar is new; the brackets are old. Chalked small on the door, at a child's height: COME OUT WHEN YOU'RE YOU.",
      "Silence, and then a knock back from inside, the same knock as yours, exactly."],
    "hall": ["No answer. Somebody inside says, very quietly: not tonight.", "Barred from inside. Through the door, somebody is reading names aloud.",
      "A slot opens at eye height, looks at you a long while, and shuts."],
    "office": ["Locked. A card in the glass: FREIGHT PAID AT THE FAR GATE. FEES PAYABLE REGARDLESS.", "Nobody in. Ledgers floor to ceiling, and one chair."],
    "store": ["Shut till the next crew's in. A card in the window: LAMPS. POWDER. EXTINGUISHERS. KITS. ASK THE CLERK.",
      "Locked. Through the window, a whole shelf of engineering kits, every one labelled with a crew's name."]
  },

  // Inside an open house: what else there is to look at, and the stair door (the upstairs isn't for the crew).
  "rooms": {
    "stairs": ["The stair door's shut. Upstairs, someone walks to the end of the room, stops, and walks back.",
      "The stair door's bolted on this side. Something on the other side has been trying it for a long time.",
      "The stair door won't open. From up the stairs, a child's voice: not yet."],
    "stove": ["A cast iron range, banked low. The kettle on it is warm. Everything in here smells of smoke and fish and wet wool.",
      "The kitchen range, its firebox door open a crack. Someone's been burning letters."],
    "photo": ["A photograph of the family on the wharf before the fall. Somebody's face has been scratched out of it with a pin. Very neatly.",
      "A wedding photograph. The groom's face has been turned to the wall by somebody cutting it out and gluing it on backwards.",
      "A school photograph, rows of children. Every child in the back row has been circled in pencil."]
  },

  // A town walled in for fear of the outside, and what becomes of those who rarely leave it (the director, 8 Oct 2026:
  // "parks, signs of governance, signs of culture, statues, things that tell the story of a people walled in for fear of
  // the outside world and what becomes of those who rarely leave the walls. They'd be trying to find ways of making the
  // world feel tolerable"; ARCHITECTURE §8 note 353). Hints, never the rules: these are a people, not a manual.
  //
  // The council's ordinances: four are posted by the clerk's door.
  "laws": [
    "The outer gate opens for the train and shuts behind it. Nobody holds it.",
    "Masks are worn out of doors, and at any window that opens.",
    "Every house answers the census bell. A house that does not answer is visited by two of the watch.",
    "Whoever comes in from outside sleeps the first night in the quiet house.",
    "Nobody is named at the gate.",
    "Lamps are lit at the fourth bell and put out at the first. The oil is the town's.",
    "No child is taken up onto the wall.",
    "What is found outside is left outside.",
    "The dead are buried inside the walls. There is room.",
    "Nobody goes out alone, and nobody goes out but by the train.",
    "The sky on the walls is painted again every spring, by everyone."
  ],

  // The pieces of a walled town's green, its flag and its murals, by kind: what each is called and what looking at it
  // tells you.
  "civic": {
    "statue": [
      { "title": "the Founder", "text": "A bronze man with one hand up, palm out, the way you'd stop somebody at a door. FOUNDER, the plinth says, and the year the wall went up. His hand is polished bright where people touch it going past." },
      { "title": "the Lamplighter", "text": "A woman in stone holding a lamp up over her head. Her face has been chipped smooth. A real lamp has been wired into the stone one, and somebody keeps it filled." },
      { "title": "the Child", "text": "A bronze child looking up at where the sun would be. Coins at its feet, though nobody's spent money here in years. A note under a stone: FOR WHEN IT COMES BACK." },
      { "title": "the Railwayman", "text": "A man in a railway coat, life size, his back to the gate. They turned him round the second winter: the old bolt holes show where he used to face the other way." }
    ],
    "memorial": [
      { "title": "the wall of names", "text": "Slate, a name cut in each, hundreds, the newest painted on because the mason's up there too. TO THOSE WHO WENT OUT. Along the bottom, a row of blank slates, already mounted." },
      { "title": "the wall of names", "text": "Names, and beside some a small iron ring. The ring means they were seen again, after. Under the heading somebody has scratched AND TO THOSE WHO CAME BACK WRONG, and somebody else has tried to scratch it out." }
    ],
    "bandstand": [
      { "title": "the bandstand", "text": "An eight-sided bandstand, its ceiling painted as a sky: blue, a white sun, gulls. A slate on the steps: THE BAND PLAYS AT THE CHANGE OF THE WATCH. LOUD, BY ORDER." },
      { "title": "the bandstand", "text": "Music stands still set out, the same sheet on every one: a reel with half its bars inked over. The chairs face the wall, not the green." }
    ],
    "garden": [
      { "title": "the lamp garden", "text": "A raised bed under lamps on poles, lit all night: kale, beans, a row of something pale. A slate: THE LAMPS ARE THE SUN NOW. TURN THEM AT NOON." },
      { "title": "the lamp garden", "text": "Glass cloches over seedlings, a candle stub by each. The labels say what they'll be: TOMATO. ROSE. ORANGE. Nothing in the bed is any of those." },
      { "title": "the tin garden", "text": "Flowers cut from tin and painted, planted in real soil in neat rows. The soil's damp. Somebody waters them." }
    ],
    "tree": [
      { "title": "a tin tree", "text": "A tree riveted out of tin, its leaves painted green. Some leaves are newer than others: the children paint a new one each in what they call spring." },
      { "title": "the old elm", "text": "A real tree, long dead, its branches hung with paper leaves and little lamps. A rope swing, its seat worn pale." }
    ],
    "flag": [
      { "title": "the town's flag", "text": "A flag on a tall pole: the wall, a lamp, the year. It comes down at the census bell and goes back up when every house has answered. Tonight it's up." },
      { "title": "the town's flag", "text": "The town's flag, a lamp on blue. Half the blue has been patched with a different blue. Nobody remembers the first one's colour now; they argue about it." }
    ],
    "mural": [
      { "title": "a painted sunrise", "text": "A sunrise across the wall, ten metres of it: a yellow sun, a blue sea, a white house on a hill. In chalk underneath, in a child's hand: THIS IS WHAT IT LOOKS LIKE." },
      { "title": "a painted window", "text": "A window painted on the stone, its shutters open on a field and a summer sky. Real curtains hang either side of it, tied back." },
      { "title": "the day", "text": "A sky painted the length of the wall, morning at one end and evening at the other, so the children can walk the day. The evening end's been painted over darker, more than once." }
    ]
  },

  // A walled town's works by its trade (queue #90, note 353; the industries' keys, then "any"): the pieces in the order
  // they stand along the line, what each is called and what looking at it tells you. "after": stands that far along the
  // line from the piece before it (the winding house from its headframe: the ropes run between them).
  "works": {
    "coal": [
      { "kind": "tip", "title": "the tip", "text": "Shale from under the town, heaped against the wall's inside. It's taller every year. The children are told not to climb it. There are paths to the top anyway, and a shirt tied to a stick up there like a flag." },
      { "kind": "headframe", "title": "{town} Pit", "text": "They sank their own shaft in the second winter, inside the wall, so nobody would have to go out to the seam. The cage is down. The bell beside the shaft is tied off so it can't ring." },
      { "kind": "coal", "title": "the town's coal", "text": "Coal from under the houses, heaped for the ranges and the train. In some kitchens you can hear the picks under the floor at night. There's no night shift.", "after": 11, "across": -6.5 },
      { "kind": "winding", "title": "the winding house", "text": "The engine that turns the drum, warm through the bricks. A chalk tally by the door: the shifts gone down and the shifts come up. The columns are the same length. Somebody has gone over them twice to be sure.", "after": 21.9 }
    ],
    "farm": [
      { "kind": "glasshouse", "title": "a glasshouse", "text": "Beans and kale under lamps, the glass whitewashed so the light doesn't get out. A rota on the door: who turns the lamps, who waters, who sits with them overnight. Nobody leaves the plants alone in the dark." },
      { "kind": "glasshouse", "title": "a glasshouse", "text": "Potatoes in sacks of soil under a low lamp, the warmest place in the town. Somebody has left a chair and a blanket in the corner, and a book open face down." },
      { "kind": "glasshouse", "title": "the seed house", "text": "Shelves of seed in tins, each labelled with the year and the bed. The oldest tins are labelled in a hand nobody here writes. Those they don't open." },
      { "kind": "elevator", "title": "the elevator", "text": "The town's grain, kept against the year the train doesn't come. The gallery light is on all night. Somebody sits up there and listens to the grain settle." },
      { "kind": "cellar", "title": "the root cellar", "text": "A turf mound with a door in it, padlocked. Inside, by the smell, turnips. A notice on the door: COUNTED. Under it, in another hand: COUNTED AGAIN." }
    ],
    "foundry": [
      { "kind": "casting", "title": "the casting shed", "text": "The cupola's lit and the windows are orange all night. They cast what the town needs: pans, hinges, firebars, the bell. They never let the fire go out. The last time it went out, the bell they cast didn't ring true, and they hung it anyway. The stack is the tallest thing inside the wall; the children say it's how the town breathes out." },
      { "kind": "slag", "title": "the slag", "text": "Slag tipped out still glowing, crusted over in black glass. In the cold it ticks, like something settling." },
      { "kind": "pigs", "title": "pig iron", "text": "Pig iron stacked like loaves, each stamped with the town's mark and a number. The numbers go back years. A few are missing, and their gaps are left in the stack." }
    ],
    "any": [
      { "kind": "warehouse", "title": "the warehouse", "text": "Where what the train brings is kept and what it takes is counted out. Chalked on the door, what came last: salt, lamp oil, nails, and one crate unmarked. NOT OPENED." },
      { "kind": "watertower", "title": "the water tower", "text": "The town's water, from the well under the square, pumped up by hand at every bell. A waterline is painted round the tank, and under it, in white: ENOUGH." }
    ]
  },

  // What the people of a walled town say of living inside it.
  "walled": [
    "My youngest was born inside. She asked me once if the train goes anywhere, or only comes.",
    "Eleven years I've not been past the gate. I don't miss it. I miss wanting to.",
    "We paint the sky on the walls so the children know which way is up.",
    "You get used to the walls. Then you get used to being used to them. That's the bad part.",
    "Some nights I put my ear to the wall, to hear that it's still there, outside. It always is.",
    "The council says the outside's smaller every year. I don't know what they measure it with.",
    "When you come back in, don't tell us what it's like. We had a man who told us. We had to let him go.",
    "The band plays at the change of the watch. So the wall knows we're still here.",
    "We grow beans under the lamps. Turn them at noon, the slate says. Nobody knows who wrote it.",
    "Children here draw the sun with a handle on it.",
    "I keep a jar of sea water on the sill. It's gone green. I don't open it.",
    "The quiet house had somebody in it last week. The bowl on the step was empty every morning. Then it wasn't."
  ]
}
